 {"id":"CF-074","title":"20 of 22 complete rows have no closure-evidence entry a stranger can resolve — the doctrine has no gate, so each closure mints a fresh unresolvable digest","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the review lane (tick code-factory-review-2026-11-03-14-58-22) in a throwaway clone of origin/main at 64c0c10828525e091a3a8747a38fd8d2e299a81e, board read at 58 rows / 22 complete.\n\nThe repository has a written closure doctrine (docs/traceability-doctrine.md, rules 2/4/6: evidence is a produced artifact, a closing verdict binds to a PUBLISHED commit, and every exact repository-relative artifact must resolve in the published tree; plus the board's own CF-047 reading that a 40/64-hex value which `git cat-file -t` does not resolve is not evidence). Nothing enforces it. tests/selftest.sh section 7 parses the JSONL and stops there; `grep -rln 'ch:trace' scripts tests` matches only selftest.sh, and no script validates detail.evidence. The result is a per-closure drift that today covers almost every closed row:\n\nA mechanical audit of every COMPLETE row's detail.evidence (script and full transcript reproduced in the lane evidence file, /home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/review/code-factory/review-2026-11-03-evidence.md) finds only TWO complete rows with an evidence entry a stranger can resolve in a fresh clone: CF-034 and CF-055. The other 20 of 22 are unresolvable, and not for exotic reasons:\n\n1. One single fingerprint, 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec, is pasted across EIGHT closures (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035). `git cat-file -t` on it prints \"fatal: Not a valid object name\"; no document anywhere in the tree states the algorithm that would regenerate it (`grep -rn tree_fp --include='*.md'` returns no definition). Eight closures therefore rest on one value that resolves to nothing and cannot be recomputed.\n2. Five more complete rows carry a 64-hex tree_fp that names no git object and is named by no pending row: CF-012 (690b81d713dc…), CF-033 second entry, CF-039 (8b3e799f6ddc…), and CF-032's first entry. (CF-036/CF-037/CF-038/CF-041/CF-052/CF-054/CF-058/PERF-012 are the same class but are already named one-by-one by CF-045/046/047/059/067/073.)\n3. CF-032's second evidence entry lists NO artifacts at all (artifacts: []) and its run_id 0576a54ecb90ce3155df1fdc984a38cf7836e92a resolves only in the owner's canonical checkout through the local ref refs/gitreins/history — a fresh clone of the published repo prints \"fatal: Not a valid object name\". Doctrine rule 4 disqualifies exactly that: a local unpushed ref does not close a finding. (This also falsifies one sentence in CF-046's reasoning, which cites 0576a54e as the published exemplar; evidence appended to CF-046.)\n4. CF-011/CF-013 list a pruned absolute host path (CF-045's class) together with an unresolvable fingerprint.\n\nThe pattern matters more than any single row: each of the individual rows named above was closed by a lane that ran a real test (the substance is often sound — this tick independently confirmed the CF-054 fix and its verdict), and then recorded a fingerprint that no reviewer can resolve. Filing one row per instance produces 20 near-duplicate rows and leaves the generator running. This row is the generator: the missing invariant and its gate. Wiring the audit into tests/selftest.sh makes a re-run prove both the current backlog and any future closure.\n\nIndependent checks run this tick at b430bed: `bash tests/selftest.sh` -> SELFTEST: PASS (rc=0, 2.50 s); gitreins guard Tier 1 DEGRADED PASS (board-only change, secrets clean). The audit script used here exits 1 today and is reproduced verbatim in the lane evidence file so the implementer can adopt it rather than re-derive it.","acceptance_criteria":"1. A committed, re-runnable audit script (e.g. scripts/closure_audit.py) takes the board path and a published ref and exits 1 while any COMPLETE row has no evidence entry whose artifacts resolve in `git ls-tree -r <ref>` AND whose hex tree_fp resolves via `git cat-file -t` to a git object and is not all zeros; it exits 0 when every complete row has one such entry. Re-run today at origin/main it must exit 1 naming at least the 20 row ids in this row's reasoning (CF-009,010,011,012,013,014,015,030,032,033,035,036,037,038,039,041,052,054,058,PERF-012); after the amendments it must exit 0. Paste both invocations and both exit codes into the evidence artifact.\n\n2. The audit is wired into tests/selftest.sh as its own numbered section and the wiring is proven causal: with the pre-fix board (origin/main at b430bed, or a copy of it) the section prints FAIL naming >=20 row ids and the run exits nonzero; with the amended board it prints PASS. Record both runs, including the section's own output lines, in the evidence artifact.\n\n3. The shared fingerprint 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec is retired the documented way for all eight rows that carry it (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035): each row either gains an appended evidence entry whose fingerprint `git cat-file -t <fp>` resolves to a tree, or records `none:<reason>` PLUS the exact command (algorithm) that regenerates the value at HEAD and the value it regenerates to. A bare re-paste of the same unresolvable digest does not satisfy this criterion.\n\n4. CF-032's second evidence entry stops resting on an unpublished ref: its run_id either names a commit that `git cat-file -t` resolves in a FRESH clone of origin (not only via refs/gitreins/history in the owner's checkout) or is superseded by an appended entry with `none:<reason>`, and its empty artifacts[] is replaced by at least one repository-relative artifact that `git cat-file -e <ref>:<path>` resolves.\n\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the closing commit, and `jq -c . .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` parses every line with no duplicate ids; the closing commit is pushed and the row's commit_hash names it.","created_at":"2026-11-03T15:02:06Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T15:49:59Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"223a2eab36ad3380a974a18b4e9a93341fc680782194f5fb5fdf9fcf27b83269"},"foreman_note":"REVIEW 2026-11-03 15:39 (code-factory-review-2026-11-03-15-39-07) — the first repair in this class landed (91f3a64, sync lane) and it still fails the rule: CF-011 and CF-013 each gained a second evidence entry (run_id sync-20261103-cf011 / sync-20261103-cf013) whose tree_fp is e3f26bdf69b3b8e65f8de6dff285dc95097c436a7dcef786e4d88a8b0a6ee09b, and `git cat-file -t` answers \"fatal: Not a valid object name\" in a fresh clone of origin/main. The artifact path it adds (docs/audits/code-factory-sync-2026-11-03-evidence-rebind.md) does resolve, and that doc states the correct tree e0e7d250bcf9661cd924ab82fa586e419866081d == d4eec41^{tree}, so the right value was known and not written. e3f26bdf is not the tree of d4eec41, e21f2ce, 91f3a64 or 6af06ef, nor sha256 of the d4eec41 tree bytes. The rebind also keeps the original unresolvable entry on purpose, so a gate worded \"any complete row lacks a resolvable entry\" cannot pass CF-011/CF-013 until the historical entry is retired (with a note saying why) or the gate is worded per-entry. Implement the gate per-entry, with an explicit retired-entry allowance, and make the red arm cover a NEW entry carrying an unresolvable fingerprint."}
 {"id":"CF-075","title":"P2 — node-create.sh stages project-node.sh without scripts/disk_floor.py, so every documented node creation dies on its first line with a raw python3 error (and leaves the spawned agent alive)","status":"complete","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory-dogfood lane at origin/main b430bed. scripts/node-create.sh:43-47 copies exactly three files into the node (~/node-scripts/project-node.sh, lib.sh, harness/install-<h>.sh); scripts/project-node.sh:64 resolves the disk floor by running \"$HERE/disk_floor.py\", which is a script added after that staging list was written, so a node never receives it. Live run on a reachable host: `BUNKER_SERVER=bunker-las-03 scripts/node-create.sh --project cf-dogfood-1100 --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness hermes --ttl 1h` -> `PASS spawn — node-cf-dogfood-1100-100225`, `PASS staged — project-node.sh + harness/install-hermes.sh`, then inside the node `python3: can't open file '/home/bunker-node-cf-dogfood-1100-100225/node-scripts/disk_floor.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory` / `NODE: FAIL (disk floor)` / `FAIL provision — node reported FAIL` / `FAIL gateway_reachable ... -> 000000` / `VERIFY: FAIL (1)` / `NODE INCOMPLETE (2)`, rc=1. The identical failure reproduces with no bunker host at all by staging exactly node-create's file set into a temp dir (see probe), so the documented create path cannot produce a working node at this tip regardless of host. The message also breaks the repo's own output convention (AGENTS.md conventions: 'PASS/FAIL/WARN with the measured value'): project-node.sh:63-68 does `say \"$FLOOR\"` and prints the raw interpreter error, so the disk-floor layer never emits a named FAIL row. Second-order defect on the same path: the failed create leaves the spawned agent ALIVE — node-create.sh:63-71 reports FAIL and exits 1 without destroying it; this tick had to run `bunker destroy node-cf-dogfood-1100-100225 --server bunker-las-03` by hand (home 246.7 MB) and a follow-up `bunker info` census returned not_found. Distinct from CF-056 (node-create stage-2 cannot FAIL because cp exit statuses are swallowed): here every cp succeeds and the staged SET is incomplete, so an exit-status fix alone would not notice this. Distinct from CF-041 (quick-start env/arg defects, closed) and CF-040 (build cache).","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree from the repo root: the `probe` field prints `python3: can't open file '.../disk_floor.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory` followed by `NODE: FAIL (disk floor)` and exits 1 today.\n1. node-create.sh stages every file project-node.sh executes at runtime — at minimum disk_floor.py — and the staged set is derived rather than hand-maintained (copy the scripts the entry point references, or stage scripts/ as a unit). A check on the staged set exits nonzero when any referenced file is missing; `grep -c disk_floor.py scripts/node-create.sh` is non-zero and a selftest fixture fails if it ever returns to 0.\n2. The hermetic staging probe continues past the disk floor and prints a NAMED row for it (PASS or WARN with the measured GiB and its source), never a bare interpreter error, and the run reaches the remaining layers.\n3. A live node creation against a reachable host shows no 'can't open file' line; its fail structure matches the control-host run (crier_identity and inference-key rows may still FAIL — this row is about staging, not about a node being complete).\n4. A failed provision leaves no live agent behind: node-create.sh destroys the agent it spawned when the run ends FAIL, or prints the exact `bunker destroy <id> --server <host>` command it requires; a post-failure `bunker info <id> --server <host>` census shows not_found or the named command is in the output.\n5. tests/selftest.sh gains a fixture that stages node-create's file set into a temp dir and asserts no unhandled interpreter error plus the named disk-floor row; `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` at the fixed tip.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/harness\" \"$d/home\"; cp scripts/project-node.sh scripts/lib.sh \"$d/\"; cp scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh \"$d/harness/\"; chmod +x \"$d/project-node.sh\"; HOME=\"$d/home\" \"$d/project-node.sh\" --apply --project cfprobe --harness hermes --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore; echo rc=$?","created_at":"2026-11-03T15:12:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T16:20:18Z","commit_hash":"77caa02876732b83d87dc2ac7c509b0e535b25b3","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"Staged set is derived and validated: node-create.sh now stages project-node.sh, lib.sh, disk_floor.py and harness/install-<h>.sh, and fails closed when project-node.sh references an unstaged $HERE file. The disk-floor layer emits a counted FAIL row instead of a raw interpreter error, and a failed provision prints the explicit --server destroy command for the agent it spawned (never for a reused one). Proved by selftest section 16 (six rows) plus the row probe before/after. Live-host creation not exercised.","detail":{"evidence":[{"run_id":"ad52e74b","ts":"2026-11-03T16:20:18Z","tree_fp":"26476812aeab71241b95be8b132d9673e7cba134a088674bed492f9f6afb2d86","artifacts":["docs/audits/code-factory-foreman-2026-11-03-cf075.probe.txt"]}]}}
